2026-09-26 — K01b: observe the actually executed target, not reread code bytes.

The fixed-address wrapper includes instruction-publish-alias.c unchanged. It
only selects MAP_FIXED_NOREPLACE addresses and prints the publication address.
The aligned direct-B protocol, immutable return targets, cache maintenance,
sync-core, retirement rwlock, 512 rounds and 20-second bound remain unchanged.
This is a GCC-built diagnostic, not an EvoCC language or sched_ext acceptance.

Build in the existing sealed ARM64 image, network disabled:
  gcc-14 -static -fno-pie -no-pie -O2 -Wall -Wextra -Werror -pthread
    tests/fixtures/instruction-publication-fixed.c -o gcc-continuous
Select the other five modes with executable-name symlinks; see included main().
All six native controls PASS. nm -S gives the observed ELF addresses:
  published         004b19f0, size 4
  publication_main  00400c44, size 069c
  install_code      00400920, size 00b4
Do not reuse these addresses with a different ELF.

The host observer builds against the installed QEMU 11.1.1 API-7 header:
  clang -std=c11 -O2 -Wall -Wextra -Werror -dynamiclib
    -undefined dynamic_lookup -I/opt/homebrew/Cellar/qemu/11.1.1/include
    $(pkg-config --cflags glib-2.0)
    tests/fixtures/instruction-publication-plugin.c -o observer-target.dylib
Add to the existing diagnostic TCG flight (not the locked acceptance contract):
  -plugin file=PATH/observer-target.dylib,pub=4b19f0,mainlo=400c44,mainhi=4012e0,patchlo=400920,patchhi=4009d4
  -d plugin
The plugin makes no guest writes, register changes or cache flushes. Writer
memory callbacks observe completed 32-bit stores in the two named functions.
Execution callbacks observe entry into immutable return-one targets at
300000000c or 300010000c while the completed code store is 14000001 (branch to
return zero) and published is zero. The read lock keeps publication stable
during the call. The plugin is bounded to this ELF/protocol, not a general
guest-address attribution mechanism.

                                GCC kernel        EvoCC kernel
Fixed wrapper, plain MT-TCG       3 PASS / 3 FAIL   3 PASS / 3 FAIL
Target observer, MT-TCG           3 PASS / 3 FAIL   4 PASS / 2 FAIL
Target observer, ST-TCG           6 PASS / 0 FAIL   6 PASS / 0 FAIL
All six final/plain boots and orderly shutdowns PASS. Same Images, payload,
4 CPUs and Cortex-A72 in all these flights; only diagnostic threading/observer
settings differ. The observer perturbs timing; one alias+ISB lucky PASS is not
a repair. Three memory-gate/locked-MOV/handshake controls pass in every flight.

MT target observer: guest missed-publication counts in program order:
  GCC kernel:   27392, 40512, 128320, 0, 0, 0 = 196224
  EvoCC kernel: 135744, 184640, 0, 0, 0, 0   = 320384
Host target-entry witnesses match these sums EXACTLY: 196224 / 320384.
Both observe 9733 completed relevant stores. ST-TCG has zero guest misses,
zero target witnesses and zero entry-sample observations on both kernels.
This localizes the bounded discrepancy to TCG executing the wrong return path
after the disabling publication, on both kernels. It does not locate the exact
invalidation race or prove the original sched_ext NULL fault has this cause.
K01/K01b remain OPEN; no original failure, limit or full-run numerator changes.

Important evidence correction: qemu_plugin_insn_data calls translator_st,
which can reread guest bytes at translation completion. Its sampled word is
NOT proof of the word originally decoded. The first observer version relied
on entry samples, missed one reference failure and is retained only as an
incomplete experiment. Current target-entry witnesses do not use that sampled
entry word as their proof. Versioned sources inspected:
  https://raw.githubusercontent.com/qemu/qemu/v11.1.1/plugins/api.c
  https://raw.githubusercontent.com/qemu/qemu/v11.1.1/accel/tcg/translator.c
The earlier invalidation-window hypothesis remains a hypothesis, not a fix.

Capture: .build/floating-targeted/instruction-plugin-20260926/
replay.py reuses tools/k1/selftest.py.flight and the retained matched Images.
The 1585664-byte payload and small host plugin reuse the existing 64 MiB scratch
disk; it is unchanged after every flight. No runtime disk or Linux clone added.
Host and sealed C compile with -Werror; gate-changed against 512ee1b correctly
selects no Rust crates for these standalone diagnostics. Renderer/check passes.

